WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.433
Sets.

2
00:00:01.733 --> 00:00:07.333
A set removes duplicates automatically, and lets you compare collections with simple operators.

3
00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:11.267
A set is an unordered bag of unique items.

4
00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:14.033
Building one removes repeats instantly.

5
00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:17.367
And checking membership is fast, because the set hashes.

6
00:00:18.567 --> 00:00:20.267
First, why sets are fast.

7
00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:24.367
Asking whether an item is in a list means scanning it one by one.

8
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:29.067
A set hashes the item and jumps straight to the answer, however big the set is.

9
00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:32.353
Now the real power: set algebra.

10
00:00:32.353 --> 00:00:34.700
Two surveys saw different asteroids.

11
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.667
Ampersand gives the intersection: objects both surveys saw.

12
00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:42.400
The pipe gives the union: five unique objects overall.

13
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.900
Minus gives the difference: seen by A and not by B.

14
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.433
And caret gives objects seen by exactly one survey.

15
00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:51.933
Here it is in Python.

16
00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:54.167
Curly braces build the two sets.

17
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:58.333
Each operator reads aloud: both, either, or only in the first.

18
00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:02.733
And wrapping a list of ids in set leaves just three unique values.

19
00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:06.000
Here's how it pays off in a RAG system.

20
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.933
Keyword search and vector search run in parallel, each returning chunk ids.

21
00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:15.611
Intersection finds chunks both retrievers loved, worth boosting.

22
00:01:15.611 --> 00:01:17.800
Union gives a deduplicated list.

23
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.967
So you never pay the reranker twice for the same chunk.

24
00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:24.133
One catch: sets have no order.

25
00:01:24.433 --> 00:01:28.600
If order matters, dedupe with list of dict dot fromkeys instead.

26
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:30.618
Let's recap.

27
00:01:30.618 --> 00:01:32.867
Sets hold unique, hashable items.

28
00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:35.067
Membership checks are constant time.

29
00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:39.967
The four operators do intersection, union, difference, and symmetric difference.

30
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:42.051
Just remember, no order.

31
00:01:42.051 --> 00:01:43.167
Next up: loops.
